error: output port `o` is driven, in a body whose every `clk.tick().await` is a bare statement and that crosses more than one clock edge per iteration, by a write whose timing the synthesized form cannot show, or whose synthesized form is a latch. On that path the form follows the write's shape and operand, never the reads before it: a conditional write that covers every path of its segment, or that no input read precedes, becomes a combinational mux assigned only inside its clock phase (a latch outside it, which Verilator refuses); a value through a same-segment `let` wire, or a conditional write behind an input read, is sampled at the segment's closing clock edge; a register, an input or a constant written unconditionally is a continuous assign that shows the committed value at every observation. The simulator runs the write at the closing edge when an input read with a `clk.tick().await` after it precedes the write on the path, and at the segment's opening otherwise — so a sampled write with no such read on some path shows one observation LATE in the hardware, an input through the continuous assign is tracked every cycle where the simulator sampled it once per iteration, and a register or constant written behind such a read shows one observation EARLY whenever its value can change at another edge (a register assigned in another segment, or after the write; a constant in a segment after the first). Measured (2026-10-30, §10.7; 2026-11-02, §10.8). Make the timing match the form: for a sampled write, read an input before every write of `o` in its segment; for a register, update it in the same segment before the write, or move the read past the `clk.tick().await`; for a conditional write, compute the value into a `let` and write it unconditionally; or declare `o` as `RegOut<…>` (a registered output, immune because it commits at the clock edge). If this module exists to DEMONSTRATE the divergence, opt out explicitly with `#[hardware(sequential, allow_pretick_alignment)]`.
